# Go
/backend/bin/
*.test
*.out

# Android
/android-dpc/.gradle/
/android-dpc/build/
# Every Gradle MODULE's output, not just `app`'s. This was `/android-dpc/app/build/` until a second
# module (`fixture-app`, whose APK the managed-install tests install) appeared and its build
# directory turned up as 60-odd untracked files in the first `git status` after it was added. A
# per-module line is a list that has to be maintained by whoever adds the next module, and the cost
# of forgetting is build output in the history.
/android-dpc/*/build/
# The Kotlin compiler's incremental-build state. Written by every Gradle invocation, machine-local,
# and large enough that committing it once makes every later diff unreadable.
/android-dpc/.kotlin/
/android-dpc/local.properties
/android-dpc/.idea/
*.iml
*.apk
# ...except the signed fixtures the APK reader's own tests parse. They are checked in because the
# tests READ them — `apk_test.go` fails with "no such file" without them — and because regenerating
# them needs `apksigner` and a keystore, neither of which a Go CI job has. The global `*.apk` above
# had already swallowed them once: `git add` staged the two `.idsig` sidecars and silently left the
# archives behind, which is a tree that passes locally, where the files exist, and fails in CI with
# an error naming a regeneration script CI cannot run.
!/backend/internal/apk/testdata/*.apk
*.aab
*.keystore
*.jks

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# Local / secrets — never commit.
# The key patterns are not theoretical: the e2e suite generates an RSA keypair for its local OIDC
# issuer, and APK_CERT_PATH points at a signing certificate. Both live in the working tree while the
# suite runs, so one `git add .` is all it takes.
.env
.env.*
*.local
/.local/
LOCAL-NOTES.md
*.pem
*.key
*.p12

# Test scratch
/tests/.tmp/
